 A data layout strategy that maps multi-dimensional data (typically 3D spatial coordinates plus discrete non-spatial dimensions like time or channel) into a linear memory structure to optimize spatial locality and I/O coalescing. It solves the problem of efficiently fetching contiguous chunks of volumetric or splat-based data by ensuring that voxels close in Euclidean space are stored in adjacent memory locations. Unlike simple row-major linearization, it uses a space-filling curve (SFC) for spatial sorting within discrete slices, and unlike pure SFC indexing, it hierarchically nests discrete non-spatial dimensions as the most significant sorting keys. See [Spatial Indexing and Compound Ordering](3096-spatial-indexing-and-compound-ordering.md).
 
+### Block ID Assignment for Composite Time Ordering
+An algorithm to assign unique integer IDs to data blocks to determine the rendering/compositing order, ensuring correct depth ordering during parallel volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. The ID assignment starts at $(0,0,0)$ with ID $1$, then increments along the $X$ axis, then $Y$, then $Z$ [S1, unofficial]. These IDs are used to sort the returned partial images from processors before compositing them into the final image [S1, unofficial]. The process iterates through blocks in the $x$-direction, incrementing the ID by $1$ for each subsequent block until no more blocks exist in the current $y$-$z$ plane [S1, unofficial]. It then resets $x$ to $0$, increments $y$ to the next block, and repeats the $x$-direction iteration until the entire $y$-dimension is covered [S1, unofficial]. Finally, it resets $x$ and $y$ to $0$, increments $z$ to the next block, and repeats the process until all blocks in the $3D$ grid have an ID [S1, unofficial]. Each partial image is assigned the ID corresponding to its source data block for composite time ordering [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Spatial vs Data Domain Indexed Point Computation
+Two variants for computing indexed points: Spatial domain computation (filters out false positives outside spatial neighborhoods) and Data domain computation (creates more spread-out signatures) [S2]. Data domain computation creates signatures that are more spread-out, selecting more noisy volume samples which are false positives [S2]. Spatial domain computation is more reliable and robust for data with spatial embedding, whereas data domain computation introduces more noisy volume samples (false positives) [S2].
